{
  "_comment": "One emblem per long-distance trail (#1543). This file is a SPECIFICATION, not artwork: the client draws each emblem from `shape`, `ground`, `figure` and `letters` the same way the map draws blaze colour from a MapLibre match expression - data-driven, one rule, no per-trail rendering code. Nothing here embeds anybody's logo file.",
  "_mark_state": {
    "org_mark_not_asked": "The emblem follows a mark its steward owns and NOBODY HAS ASKED THEM. This is the honest value for most rows and it is deliberately greppable. It matches the value export_sources.py already publishes per source, where the whole registry reads not_asked today - see test_says_no_organization_has_licensed_a_mark. A club inheriting this project needs to settle these in its own name.",
    "public_domain": "A US Government or state agency trail marker, which is not a private mark.",
    "own_work": "No third-party mark exists; the emblem is OurHike's own, drawn from the blaze."
  },
  "_shapes": {
    "rectangle": "A painted blaze. The default, and what most of these trails actually use.",
    "diamond": "A diamond blaze or diamond marker.",
    "triangle": "A triangular marker.",
    "circle": "A round medallion.",
    "shield": "A routed or cast sign, the federal and agency idiom.",
    "arrowhead": "An arrowhead marker.",
    "dotted-circle": "OurHike's own, for a route with no maintainer. The dots say unmaintained."
  },
  "_blaze_note": "`blaze` is the trail's painted blaze where it has one and 'none' where it is signed rather than blazed. It is NOT decoration and it is NOT the emblem's ground colour by accident: features/TRAIL_BLAZE_COLORS.md's rule is that a blaze colour in a map or a table is data. Where the two agree, they agree because the mark follows the blaze.",
  "_why_this_exists": "features/TRAIL_BLAZE_COLORS.md records that line WIDTH carries the hue-independent channel - a through-route is drawn wider than the spurs off it - and says outright that this 'answers it for exactly as long as that stays true', because 'a second through-route on the map turns the question width answers into through-route or spur'. The 11 new endpoints in features/ORG_BULK_LOAD.md are that second through-route arriving, several times over. An emblem is what answers WHICH through-route once width can only say THAT it is one.",
